<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aretas IV</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 9 BCE–40 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Aretas IV Philopatris</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aretas IV was king of the Nabataeans, whose long reign marked the height of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petra">Petra</a>'s prosperity and building. His daughter married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-antipas">Herod Antipas</a>, tetrarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a>, who divorced her to marry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodias">Herodias</a>; Aretas defeated Antipas in a war in 36 CE. He is mentioned by Paul in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aretas-iv.jpg" alt="Aretas IV" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group (CNG), Inc., www.cngcoins.com · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bronze_coin_of_Aretas_IV_from_3_BC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
